"AReaL-Hex: Accommodating Asynchronous RL Training over Heterogeneous GPUs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00796)\nciteturn2288academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Title: AReaL-Hex: Accommodating Asynchronous RL Training over Heterogeneous GPUs ... On the mathematical reasoning task with various model scales (1.5B, 7B, and 14B), compared to homogeneous deployments of state-of-the-art asynchronous RL systems: (i) When maintaining the same total budgets, AReaL-Hex delivers up to 1.50x higher training throughput; (ii) When achieving the same training throughput, AReaL-Hex results in up to 1.46x reduction in training cost.\nTitle: AReaL-Hex: Accommodating Asynchronous RL Training over Heterogeneous GPUs\nAuthors: Ran Yan, Youhe Jiang, Tianyuan Wu, Jiaxuan Gao, Zhiyu Mei, Wei Fu, Haohui Mai, Wei Wang, Yi Wu, Binhang Yuan\nDate: Sun Nov  2 04:17:30 2025\n\nMaximizing training throughput and cost-efficiency of RL for LLMs is essential to democratize this advanced technique. One promising but challenging approach is to deploy such a computational workflow over heterogeneous GPUs. Unlike conventional large-scale LLM pretraining, RL training generally decomposes into three coupled stages, i.e., rollout generation, reward computation, and policy/value updates, which exhibit markedly different compute intensities, memory footprints, and communication patterns. Recent research shows that fully asynchronous RL training can disaggregate these stages across disjoint hardware pools without sacrificing training stability, creating a great opportunity for real-world heterogeneous deployment. To this end, we present AReaL-Hex, a heterogeneity-aware asynchronous RL training system that effectively schedules how to execute rollout generation and policy model training over heterogeneous GPUs while enforcing data staleness bounds. Concretely, we use a two-phase scheduler: (i) a constrained search with MILP to select per-stage parallelization strategies and workload assignments given a resource budget, and (ii) a graph-partitioning step that allocates heterogeneous GPUs and interconnects to maximize end-to-end throughput. Built atop a fully asynchronous RL architecture, AReaL-Hex maps HBM-I/O-bound generation and compute-bound optimization to more cost-efficient resources and balances their producer-consumer interactions to avoid both idleness and stale rollout trajectories. On the mathematical reasoning task with various model scales (1.5B, 7B, and 14B), compared to homogeneous deployments of state-of-the-art asynchronous RL systems: (i) When maintaining the same total budgets, AReaL-Hex delivers up to 1.50x higher training throughput; (ii) When achieving the same training throughput, AReaL-Hex results in up to 1.46x reduction in training cost.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Learned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2511-01872.html)\nciteturn2288search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # \"Learned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware.\" ... type: Informal or Other Publication\n\n# \"Learned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nEtash Guha et al. (2025)\n\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2511.01872\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2025-11-20\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2511-01872\n\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2511-01872\n\nEtash Guha, Tianxiao Jiang, Andrew Deng, Jian Zhang, Muthu Annamalai:\nLearned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware. CoRR abs/2511.01872 (2025)\n\nlast updated on 2025-11-20 16:31 CET by the dblp team--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01872)\nciteturn2288academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Title: Learned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware\nTitle: Learned Cost Model for Placement on Reconfigurable Dataflow Hardware\nAuthors: Etash Guha, Tianxiao Jiang, Andrew Deng, Jian Zhang, Muthu Annamalai\nDate: Tue Oct 21 22:45:45 2025\n\nMapping a dataflow-graph of an ML model onto a reconfigurable system is difficult, as different mappings have different throughputs and consume resource constraints differently. To solve this, a model to evaluate the throughput of mappings is necessary as measuring throughput completely is expensive. Many use a hand-designed analytical model, relying on proxy features or intuition, introducing error. We provide a Learned Approach that predicts throughput 31%-52% more accurately over a variety of graphs. In addition, our approach shows no accuracy degradation after removing performance annotations. We show that using this approach results in 5.6% faster compiled graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFREESH: Fair, Resource- and Energy-Efficient Scheduling for LLM Serving on Heterogeneous GPUs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00807)\nciteturn2288academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Title: FREESH: Fair, Resource- and Energy-Efficient Scheduling for LLM Serving on Heterogeneous GPUs ... Date: Sun Nov  2 05:17:02 2025\nTitle: FREESH: Fair, Resource- and Energy-Efficient Scheduling for LLM Serving on Heterogeneous GPUs\nAuthors: Xuan He, Zequan Fang, Jinzhao Lian, Danny H. K. Tsang, Baosen Zhang, Yize Chen\nDate: Sun Nov  2 05:17:02 2025\n\nThe ever-increasing computation and energy demand for LLM and AI agents call for holistic and efficient optimization of LLM serving systems. In practice, heterogeneous GPU clusters can be deployed in a geographically distributed manner, while LLM load also observes diversity in terms of both query traffic and serving patterns. LLM queries running on advanced GPUs during a high-emission hour at one location can lead to significantly higher carbon footprints versus same queries running on mid-level GPUs at a low-emission time and location. By observing LLM serving requirements and leveraging spatiotemporal computation flexibility, we consider the joint routing and scheduling problem, and propose FREESH to cooperatively run a group of data centers while minimizing user-specified carbon or energy objectives. FREESH identifies the optimal configurations of balanced load serving by matching distinct GPU instance's power-throughput characteristics with predictable LLM query length and workloads. To ensure both latency and fairness requirements, FREESH identifies optimized parallelism and query routing schedules together with dynamic GPU frequency scaling for power saving, and Least-Laxity-First (LLF) serving strategy for query scheduling. During the 1-hour serving on production workloads, FREESH reduces energy by 28.6% and emissions by 45.45% together with improvements in SLO attainment and fairness.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCostream: Learned Cost Models for Operator Placement in Edge-Cloud Environments | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/382518761_Costream_Learned_Cost_Models_for_Operator_Placement_in_Edge-Cloud_Environments)\nciteturn2288search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # Costream: Learned Cost Models for Operator Placement in Edge-Cloud Environments ... Shukla et al. [21] introduce a model-driven approach for predicting stream workloads and resource allocations, considering task requirements (e.g., CPU, memory) and data rates to optimize resource utilization and costs. [12] and [2] employ zeroshot learning models in their predictive scheduling approaches. ... This research makes four distinct contributions: (1) comprehensive evaluation of quantized transformer performance across hybrid deployments, achieving up to 28.9× model size reduction while maintaining accuracy [14] and 5× kernel speedup on ARM (Advanced RISC Machines) CPUs; (2) intelligent hybrid routing using threshold-based workload partitioning guided by performance, compliance, and cost metrics, delivering sub-6ms latency for real-time applications [15]; (3) detailed cost modeling framework quantifying tradeoffs between edge capital expenditure and cloud operational costs, revealing substantial TCO (Total Cost of Ownership) reductions for large-scale contact centers [16] ; and (4) domain-adaptive pre-training for contact center sentiment analysis, demonstrating significant improvements in knowledge-intensive tasks (+8.1%) and contextual understanding (+7.6%) [17].\nConference Paper\n\n# Costream: Learned Cost Models for Operator Placement in Edge-Cloud Environments\n\n  * May 2024\n\nDOI:10.1109/ICDE60146.2024.00015\n\n  * Conference: 2024 IEEE 40th International Conference on Data Engineering (ICDE)\n\nAuthors:\n\nRoman Heinrich\n\nRoman Heinrich\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nCarsten Binnig\n\n  * Brown University\n\nHarald Kornmayer\n\nHarald Kornmayer\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nManisha Luthra\n\n  * Technical University of Darmstadt\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (14)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAReaL-Hex: Heterogeneous Async RL (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/areal-hex)\nciteturn2288search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Instead, AReaL-Hex starts from a small $$\\mathcal{D}_T \\cup \\mathcal{D}_I = \\mathcal{D}, \\qquad \\mathcal{D}_T \\cap \\mathcal{D}_I = \\emptyset,$$D T​∪D I​=D,D T​∩D I​=∅,7 and increases it until scheduling decisions stabilize (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n## 2. Architectural decomposition and asynchronous execution\n\nThe system adopts a fully asynchronous RL pipeline with three logical components: rollout workers, reward computation, and training workers (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). Rollout workers continuously sample responses from the current policy; reward computation scores those trajectories; training workers consume them to update the policy/value model. Parameter flow proceeds in the reverse direction during synchronization: once a new model version is ready, training and rollout are temporarily paused and weights are broadcast from training workers to rollout workers via NCCL collective communication (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\nAReaL-Hex explicitly partitions the cluster into disjoint hardware pools for inference and training,\n\n$$\\mathcal{D}_T \\cup \\mathcal{D}_I = \\mathcal{D}, \\qquad \\mathcal{D}_T \\cap \\mathcal{D}_I = \\emptyset,$$D T​∪D I​=D,D T​∩D I​=∅,\n\nwhere $\\mathcal{D}_I$D I​ is used for rollout generation and $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​ for policy/value updates (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). This disaggregation is the enabling condition for heterogeneous specialization. The paper characterizes rollout generation as primarily HBM-I/O-bound, whereas policy/value optimization is compute-bound and communication-intensive. Reward computation is treated as part of the rollout-side cost and is profiled as relatively stable in the scheduling model (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\nThe system’s staleness argument is architectural rather than algorithmically novel. It inherits a bounded-staleness asynchronous RL regime from AReaL, and then enforces balanced rollout/training rates so stale trajectories do not accumulate uncontrollably (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). The scheduling logic is therefore inseparable from training stability: if training is too slow, rollout workers eventually stall; if rollout is too slow, trainers idle. AReaL-Hex treats these as dual failure modes of poor hardware allocation.\n\n## 3. Scheduling formulation and the two-phase optimizer\n\nThe scheduler is given a heterogeneous GPU set $\\mathcal{D}$D, a staleness parameter $\\eta$η, and an expected rollout-length distribution $P$P, and seeks a training plan $\\sigma$σ, a rollout plan $\\tau$τ, and a partition $1.46\\times$1.46×0 minimizing the slower of the two asynchronous sides (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). Throughput is defined from average tokens processed divided by average RL training step time, where step time includes weight synchronization latency and the maximum of rollout-side and training-side duration, averaged over 30 RL training steps (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\nThe headline results are twofold. Relative to homogeneous H800 deployments, AReaL-Hex achieves up to $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​3 speedup on 14B, at least $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​4 speedup on 7B, and $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​5 speedup on average (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). Relative to homogeneous H20 deployments, it achieves up to $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​6 speedup on 7B, at least $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​7 speedup on 14B, and $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​8 average speedup (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). The paper attributes these gains to stage-hardware matching: versus H800-only, rollout latency falls; versus H20-only, training latency falls (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\nThe breakdown is consistent with that interpretation. Compared to homogeneous H800, AReaL-Hex reduces rollout-generation latency by a maximum of $\\mathcal{D}_T$D T​9, a minimum of $\\mathcal{D}$D 0, and an average of $\\mathcal{D}$D 1. Compared to homogeneous H20, it reduces model-training latency by a maximum of $\\mathcal{D}$D 2, a minimum of $\\mathcal{D}$D 3, and an average of $\\mathcal{D}$D 4 (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\nAt equal throughput, the heterogeneous system is also cheaper. For representative settings, AReaL-Hex yields about $\\mathcal{D}$D 5 lower cost for 1.5B, $\\mathcal{D}$D 6 lower cost for 7B, and $\\mathcal{D}$D 7 lower cost for 14B relative to homogeneous H800 (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). Across heterogeneous cluster sizes from 24 to 56 GPUs, the paper reports fairly stable per-dollar throughput of about 200 tokens/s/$\\mathcal{D}$8 for 7B, and 24 tokens/s/$ for 14B (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\nThe principal cost of heterogeneity is synchronization. Update times increase markedly in the mixed setting: for 1.5B, 7B, and 14B, AReaL-Hex reports 10.06 s, 58.34 s, and 112.93 s, respectively, compared with lower homogeneous values on both H800 and H20 (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). As a share of RL iteration time, weight synchronization averages 13.54% in AReaL-Hex, compared with about 1.9% in homogeneous H800 (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). The system remains net-positive because the stage-matching gains outweigh this penalty.\n\nThe ablation evidence shows that heterogeneity alone is insufficient without optimized resource partitioning. Compared with a uniform-allocation baseline AReaL(u), optimized allocation improves throughput by $\\mathcal{D}$D 9 at 1.5B, $\\eta$η 0 at 7B, and $\\eta$η 1 at 14B, for an average gain of $\\eta$η 2 (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025). On the scheduler side, replacing either phase with exhaustive search greatly increases convergence time. The full two-phase scheduler converges in 14.9 s at 24 GPUs, 23.1 s at 32 GPUs, 46.7 s at 40 GPUs, and 2.0 min at 56 GPUs, whereas ablated variants range from minutes to at least 40 minutes (Yan et al., 2 Nov 2025).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - AndrewFangZequan/LLM_Serving_FREESH · GitHub (https://github.com/AndrewFangZequan/LLM_Serving_FREESH)\nciteturn2288search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # FREESH: Fair, Resource- and Energy-Efficient Scheduling for LLM Serving on Heterogeneous GPUs\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nBert_Classifier  | Bert_Classifier  |    |  \nPoolMILP  | PoolMILP  |    |  \n.gitignore  | .gitignore  |    |  \nLICENSE  | LICENSE  |    |  \nLLF-simulation.py  | LLF-simulation.py  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \n\n## Repository files navigation\n\n  *   * README\n  * MIT license\n\nMore items\n\n# FREESH: Fair, Resource- and Energy-Efficient Scheduling for LLM Serving on Heterogeneous GPUs\n\n> A modular routing + scheduling system for LLM inference that jointly optimizes energy/carbon, SLOs, and fairness across heterogeneous, geo-distributed GPU clusters.\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * Three-layer co-design\n    * Pool-level (slow timescale): MILP-based optimizer for cross-region placement, request partitioning, and tensor-parallel (TP) mode selection with switchable energy or carbon objectives.\n    * GPU-level (seconds): MIAD (Multiplicative Increase, Additive Decrease) dynamic frequency control to reduce power while meeting SLOs.\n    * Request-level (sub-second): LLF (Least Laxity First) preemptive scheduler for fairness and lower tail latency (reduces FCFS head-of-line blocking).\n  * Six “control knobs”: site selection, partitioning, request type, execution mode (TP / model-GPU pairing), frequency (DVFS/MIAD), and scheduling policy (LLF).\n  * Carbon-intensity aware routing: leverages spatiotemporal grid carbon signals to shift work to cleaner regions/periods.\n  * Plug-and-play: integrates with common inference runtimes (e.g., vLLM) and multiple solvers (e.g., Gurobi / open-source backends).\n\n> Full method and evaluations: https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00807\n\n## About\n\nNo description, website, or topics provided.\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\nMIT license\n\nActivity\n\n### Stars\n\n8 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n1 fork--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nU.S. Patent for Iterative database driven place and