ect on a live system.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\n  * 1 file\n  * 0 forks\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuatorze: A Guide · GitHub (https://gist.github.com/jetpham/6fdd833e3b045e463096fa764a299926)\nciteturn2288search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * Freesh(فَرِش): After the deal and before any play, any player can request for a reshuffle by calling \"Freesh\". ... This minimum is raised by one pair for each joker in the hand (if you have 2 jokers, you must have at least 5 pairs of duplicate cards to require a reshuffle).\nJokers can be used as wild cards but not discarded. Jokers can be replaced in melds by the card they are representing.\n  3. \"Plus\" Rule: A card that could be used to complete an existing meld cannot be discarded. It must be used in the player's own meld or added to an opponent’s meld.\n\n## Special Rules\n\n  * Freesh(فَرِش): After the deal and before any play, any player can request for a reshuffle by calling \"Freesh\". It is required if they have a minimum of three pairs of duplicate cards (cards of the same value and suit). This minimum is raised by one pair for each joker in the hand (if you have 2 jokers, you must have at least 5 pairs of duplicate cards to require a reshuffle).\n  * Last Card: The last card discarded to end the round must be placed face down. This card cannot be used in a meld.\n  * Jokers: A joker can be kept until the end of the game. If a player has only jokers left, opponents add 100 points to their score. Jokers in melds can be replaced by the true card they represent.\n  * Completed Melds: If a meld cannot be added to anymore (e.g., all four suits are in a set or a sequence from Ace to King is formed), it is added to the discard pile underneath the top card.\n\n## Scoring\n\n  * Points in hand are added up and contributed to the player's score (with a maximum of 100).\n  * Jokers and Aces left in hand count as 10 points each.\n  * The highest cumulative score at the end of the series of rounds loses. The player with the lowest cumulative score wins.\n\n## Examples of Play\n\n  1. Initial Meld: A player has 7♥, 7♠, 7♦, and 8♠, 9♠, 10♠. The total is 51, so they may meld.\n  2. Joker in Meld: If a player has J♦ in a sequence 9♦, 10♦, J♦, a Joker can replace Q♦ if another player wants to retrieve it.\n\n## End of the Round\n\n  * Once a player discards their last card, the round ends.\n  * Card values in hand are scored and added.\n  * The dealer rotation is clockwise after each round.\n\n## Game Continuation\n\n  * The game can continue for a pre-defined number of rounds or until a score threshold is reached.\n  * Scores are kept on a leaderboard, with the lowest cumulative scores being the best.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearned Cost Model for Placement on (https://etash.me/papers/sn_website.pdf)\nciteturn2288search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.8 years ago; model-based algorithms to perform placement and routing. ... the pain points in heuristic-based cost modeling, we empir- ... cost model for placement and routing in compiling DNNs\nY = {yg, ∀g ∈{1, 2, ..., N}} where yg ∈R is the conse-\nquent throughput of PnR decision Gg. Using the throughput\nmeasurements, we learn the regressor together with the fusion\nnetwork and embeddings in an end-to-end fashion. The Adam\noptimizer is used to perform parameter updates [5].\nThis throughput regressor can be combined with any cost-\nmodel-based algorithms to perform placement and routing.\nOur throughput regressor is light-weight and could be used\nas a drop-in replacement in production-level compilers to\naccelerate real DNN training workloads.\nIV. EXPERIMENTS\nTo validate that our data-driven cost modeling can resolve\nthe pain points in heuristic-based cost modeling, we empir-\nically evaluate the throughput regressor in Section III as a\ncost model for placement and routing in compiling DNNs\nto the dataflow arc